## Style Guide

### General Principles

- Before adding complexity for a speculative or vanishingly unlikely race or security edge case, explain the concrete failure mode, likelihood, and complexity cost to the user and get their buy-in. Do not silently expand scope for theoretical robustness.
- DO NOT leak our conversation, prompt, or iteration history into code comments, pull request descriptions, or other maintainer-facing prose. No "used to be X, now Y", "changed from", "per the user's request", or similar. Comments describe the code as it is, and only when the why is non-obvious.
- Never add AI attribution to commits, PR bodies, or issues: no `Co-Authored-By` trailers, no "Generated with Claude Code" lines, and no "Co-authored with Claude" footers.

### Git signing and hooks

- Every commit must be signed and every git hook must run. Never use `--no-verify`, `-n` on `git commit`, `--no-gpg-sign`, `-c commit.gpgsign=false`, `git config commit.gpgsign false`, `GIT_CONFIG_*` overrides, or any other way to skip hooks or signing.
- If signing fails (for example, 1Password is locked or `op-ssh-sign` errors) or a hook rejects the commit or push, stop and report the exact error to the user. Do not retry with signing or hooks disabled.

### Deleting files

- Delete with `trash <path>...`, never `rm` or `find -delete`. `trash` moves files to the system trash, so they are recoverable. Pass no flags. A hook blocks `rm`.

### Git clones

- Clone with `ghq get <owner>/<repo>` (or a full URL), never `git clone`. ghq puts the clone at `~/src/<host>/<owner>/<repo>`, which the git identity depends on.
- Before cloning, run `ghq list -p <repo>` to check for an existing clone, and use it if found.

### Worktrees (worktrunk)

- Create a worktree with `wt switch --create --no-cd <branch>` and run commands with `git -C <path>` or by `cd`-ing in the command.
- To delegate a separate task, create its worktree the same way, then start `/rex-agent`-style work with the `subagent` tool and pass the worktree path as `cwd`. The result returns to you automatically. Call `rex_finish` after reviewing it to close the tab.

### Shell history (atuin)

- Every command run on this user's machines, by the user or by agents, is in atuin history with exit code, directory, duration, host, and author, synced across machines.
- Before guessing how to build, test, deploy, or run something, search it: `atuin search --cwd . --exit 0 --limit 20 <keyword>`. Results print oldest first; the last line is the newest.
- Useful filters: `--author '$all-user'` (only the user's commands), `--author '$all-agent'`, `--exclude-exit 0` (failures), `--format '{time} {exit} {directory} {command}'`. Never pass `-i` (interactive TUI).

### 1Password

- Reads use a read-only service account by default. For any `op` write (create, edit, delete), prefix the command with `OP_APP_AUTH=1`, e.g. `OP_APP_AUTH=1 op item edit ...`.
